  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\nAn upper level low meandering in the vicinity of northern CA is \nresulting in an increasingly southerly flow aloft across the \nNorthern Rockies. The first of several weaker embedded waves will \nbegin moving through this morning, resulting in an initial round of \nshowers and perhaps a couple thunderstorms across SW MT. While \nseveral additional rounds of showers and thunderstorms look to move \nacross similar areas through the day today, the strength of any \nthunderstorm that develops this afternoon will be dictated by how \nmuch instability will be able to develop between rounds of \nprecipitation. \n\nStronger forcing, as well as better thermodynamics and perhaps \nkinematics arrive for Friday as the core of the upper disturbance \napproaches. Whether or not shear will be sufficient for a more \norganized severe weather threat remains to be seen. That said, \nshould the shear be lower, that would result in a slower storm \nmotion and result in an increased risk for excessive rainfall \nleading to flash flooding given the increased precipitable water \nvalues across the region. Trends will continue to be monitored. \n\nThe core of the upper disturbance finally moves overhead Saturday \nand eventually eastward Saturday night. Additional precipitation \naccompanies this passage in most areas, though areas west of Shelby \nto the Rocky Mountain Front are looking to remain dry Saturday. \n\nAlthough the upper level pattern gets messy to close out the weekend \ninto early next week, confidence remains high in Sunday and Monday \nbeing dry and on the warm side. Thereafter confidence in the \nevolution of the pattern becomes quite low. -AM\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\nThunderstorms the next few days:\n\nWhile confidence is high that showers and thunderstorms will develop \nover the next few days, confidence in just how much instability \ndevelops remains on the lower side. Relatively strong forcing will \naccompany the passage of this system, particularly on Friday. This \nforcing may ultimately result in a convective initiation that is a \nbit too early to maximize daytime heating, resulting in ample cloud \ncover across much of the region by mid-afternoon that limits \nthunderstorm strength. -AM",
